{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>כִּי אִם זְכַרְתַּנִי</b> [if you remember me, etc.]. Perhaps he made a condition upon him, that the good interpretation would not be fulfilled except on the condition of ״אִם זְכַרְתַּנִי.״ And the reason Joseph placed his trust in him is because he saw that his dream would portend good tidings, for Israel are likened to a vine, as they say in the verse (חולין צב.) <b>וְהִנֵּה גֶפֶן לְפָנַי</b> [and behold, a vine before me] etc. And if the dream had come only concerning a matter pertaining to the chief butler, it would have been enough for HaShem to show him that he places the cup on the palm of Pharaoh; but surely He showed him concerning Joseph — that he is the vine that was before him there in the prison house. And His saying <b>וְהִיא כְפֹרַחַת</b> [and it was budding] means that the vine would bud and ascend. <b>נִצָּהּ</b> [it shot forth] means it would rise from level to level. And this is what he made known to him by way of inclusion in the interpretation, and he said to him: ״כִּי אִם זְכַרְתַּנִי״ etc." "And after the truth — so it was: through his hand Joseph was elevated, and Joseph thought that he would explain to him the matter that was said to him in the dream and reveal to him its secret. And the reason Joseph was punished is that he erred in thinking that the elevation of Joseph would come about through the will of the chief butler, and it was not so; for truthfully HaShem would bring the matter about through him but not through his will, and so it was — for against his will he spoke to Pharaoh: ״וְשָׁם אִתָּנוּ״ (Genesis 41:12). And in the vision of the dream, He made the matter known to the chief butler in order that Joseph would receive good tidings through it — not that he should place his trust in him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:19.659908596Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}